<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'

import Code from '#src/lib/Code.abide'
import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Example from '#src/lib/Example.abide'
import Exchange from '#src/lib/Exchange.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'

import AskAboutAda from './lib/AskAboutAda.abide'
import askAboutAda from './lib/AskAboutAda.abide' with { type: 'text' }

// One JSON-RPC message to the CRM's MCP endpoint, as a model's client posts it.
const rpc = (method: string, params: Record<string, unknown>) =>
    JSON.stringify({ jsonrpc: '2.0', id: 1, method, params }, null, 2)

// A transcript as the browser sends it to the CRM's assistant handler.
const transcript = (messages: { role: string; content: unknown }[]) =>
    JSON.stringify({ messages }, null, 2)
</script>

<Doc
    seeAlso={[
        '/ai/assistant',
        '/ai/provider',
        '/ai/pairing',
        '/ai/results-of',
        '/ai/assistant-message',
        '/headless/chat',
        '/handlers/mcp',
        '/handlers/clients',
        '/app/on-pairing',
        '/cli/bridge',
    ]}
>
    <Section title="Every handler the app serves is already a tool">
        <p>The model reaches the CRM through <a href={url('/handlers/mcp')}>MCP</a>, and
            <code>/__abide/mcp</code> lists each handler as a tool. The handler's
            <code>description</code> is what the model reads, and its parameter's annotation is
            the schema. You write no tool definitions. The CRM's contact search, as the tool
            list answered when this page loaded:</p>
        <Exchange
            method="POST"
            path="/__abide/mcp"
            body={rpc('tools/list', {})}
            pick={[
                '/result/tools/[name=get_assistant_searchContacts]/name',
                '/result/tools/[name=get_assistant_searchContacts]/description',
                '/result/tools/[name=get_assistant_searchContacts]/inputSchema',
            ]} />
    </Section>

    <Section title="Run the conversation from a handler you write">
        <p><a href={url('/ai/assistant')}><code>assistant</code></a> has no address of its own.
            Yield it from a streaming <code>POST</code>, so the address and the middleware are
            yours. <code>tools: true</code> hands the model the tools <code>/__abide/mcp</code>
            lists, and each call the model makes runs on the server, through the app.</p>
        <Code language="ts" caption="server" code={`
            import {
                assistant,
                type AssistantMessage,
                provider,
                rpc,
} from '@abide/abide/server'

            const claude = provider.claudeApi({
                apiKey: Bun.env.ANTHROPIC_API_KEY ?? '',
                model: 'claude-opus-5',
                max_tokens: 1024,
            })

            export default rpc(
                async function* ({ messages }: { messages: AssistantMessage[] }) {
                    yield* assistant(messages, claude, { tools: true })
                },
                { methods: ['POST'], clients: { mcp: false } },
            )
        `} />
        <p>The CRM's assistant runs the same loop on a recorded model. Asked when this page
            loaded, its tool call ran the real contact search, and the answer streamed back as
            events:</p>
        <Exchange
            method="POST"
            path="/assistant/askAssistant"
            body={transcript([{ role: 'user', content: 'Which company is Ada at?' }])} />
    </Section>

    <Section title="Show the model's tool calls in the conversation">
        <p>Hand the assistant handler to <a href={url('/headless/chat')}><code>chat</code></a>
            as <code>ask</code>. A tool part holds the call the model made and what your handler
            answered. Send the question to see the CRM's search run.</p>
        <Example code={askAboutAda}><AskAboutAda /></Example>
    </Section>

    <Section title="The model calls each tool as the person who asked">
        <p>A tool call carries the asking request's cookie and authorization, and nothing else
            of it. A model reaches what the person asking could reach, and no more. The CRM's
            commission takes <a href={url('/middleware/signed-in')}><code>signedIn</code></a>, so
            asked by nobody signed in, the tool refuses as the page would:</p>
        <Exchange
            method="POST"
            path="/__abide/mcp"
            body={rpc('tools/call', {
                name: 'get_account_getCommission',
                arguments: {},
            })} />
    </Section>

    <Section title="Keep a handler off the model's tool list">
        <p>Set <code>mcp</code> to <code>false</code> in
            <a href={url('/handlers/clients')}><code>clients</code></a>. The CRM's assistant
            handler withholds itself, so the model cannot call the assistant from inside a
            conversation. <code>clients</code> withholds a surface and guards nothing: the
            address still answers a page.</p>
        <Exchange
            method="POST"
            path="/__abide/mcp"
            body={rpc('tools/call', {
                name: 'post_assistant_askAssistant',
                arguments: {},
            })} />
    </Section>

    <Section title="Swap the model by swapping the provider">
        <p><a href={url('/ai/provider')}><code>provider</code></a> picks where a turn runs:
            <code>provider.claudeApi</code> on your key, or <code>provider.claudeCode</code> on
            the visitor's own Claude Code and their plan. A recorded
            <a href={url('/ai/assistant')}><code>Provider</code></a> with fixed lines is what the
            CRM's tests and this page run. Every one yields the same events, so the page asking
            does not change. The CRM's Claude Code handler:</p>
        <Code language="ts" caption="server" code={`
            import {
                assistant,
                type AssistantMessage,
                provider,
                rpc,
} from '@abide/abide/server'

            export default rpc(
                async function* ({ messages }: { messages: AssistantMessage[] }) {
                    yield* assistant(
                        messages,
                        provider.claudeCode({
                            systemPrompt: 'Answer in one sentence.',
                        }),
                        { tools: true },
                    )
                },
                { methods: ['POST'],
                    description: "Asks the visitor's Claude Code.",
                    clients: { mcp: false },
                },
            )
        `} />
    </Section>

    <Section title="Pair the visitor's Claude Code before the first question">
        <p><a href={url('/ai/pairing')}><code>pairing</code></a> runs in the browser, and connects
            this browser to the visitor's Claude Code through
            <a href={url('/cli/bridge')}><code>abide-bridge</code></a>. Until they pair,
            <code>provider.claudeCode</code> refuses the turn <code>409</code>, so show the chat
            only in the <code>paired</code> step.</p>
        <Code language="abide" caption="abide" code={`
            <script>
            import { pairing } from '@abide/abide'
            import askClaudeCode from '../askClaudeCode.server.ts'
            import Chat from './Chat.abide'

            const { status, step, start } = pairing()
            </script>

            {#if step === 'paired'}
                <Chat ask={askClaudeCode} />
            {:else if step === 'click'}
                <pre>{status?.command}</pre>
                <button onclick={start}>Pair with a click</button>
            {/if}
        `} />
    </Section>

    <Section title="Claude Code on the visitor's machine uses only the tools both sides name">
        <p>A paired turn runs in an empty directory, with the app's tools. A tool of the
            visitor's machine joins them only where the site's <code>tools</code> and the
            visitor's own <code>--tools</code> both name it, so asking cannot reach their files.
            A visitor who runs the bridge <code>--full</code> grants everything.
            <a href={url('/app/on-pairing')}><code>onPairing</code></a> sets what the pairing
            command passes.</p>
        <Code language="ts" caption="server" code={`
            import { provider } from '@abide/abide/server'

            const claudeCode = provider.claudeCode({ tools: ['WebSearch'] })
        `} />
        <p>A tool the site asked for and the visitor did not grant is in the pairing's
            <code>status.missing</code>:</p>
        <Code language="abide" caption="abide" code={`
            <script>
            import { pairing } from '@abide/abide'
            const { status } = pairing()
            </script>

            {#if status?.missing.length}
                <p>Claude Code here cannot use {status.missing.join(', ')}.</p>
            {/if}
        `} />
    </Section>

    <Section title="The browser keeps the conversation and sends it whole">
        <p>The server holds no conversation between calls, so each question sends every message
            before it. The conversation is the Messages API's own messages, and
            <a href={url('/ai/assistant-message')}><code>assistantMessage</code></a> turns an
            answer's events into the messages they amount to. <code>chat</code> folds them in as
            they stream.</p>
        <Code language="ts" caption="browser" code={`
            import { assistantMessage, type AssistantMessage } from '@abide/abide'
            import askAssistant from './askAssistant.server.ts'

            const messages: AssistantMessage[] = []
            async function ask(question: string) {
                messages.push(await assistantMessage('user', [question]))
                const events = await askAssistant({ messages })
                messages.push(...assistantMessage('assistant', events))
            }
        `} />
        <p>A transcript ending on the assistant's turn has nothing to answer, and is refused
            before any model is asked:</p>
        <Exchange
            method="POST"
            path="/assistant/askAssistant"
            body={transcript([
                { role: 'user', content: 'Which company is Ada at?' },
                { role: 'assistant', content: 'Analytical Engines.' },
            ])} />
    </Section>

    <Section title="Read what a tool answered as its handler's own value">
        <p>A tool's answer reaches the model as text. In a page,
            <a href={url('/headless/chat')}><code>chat</code></a>'s <code>calls(handler)</code>
            hands back each call to one handler, typed as that handler's, its value as the answer
            delivered it, live while it streams. For a conversation outside a
            <code>chat</code> — one stored and read on a server —
            <a href={url('/ai/results-of')}><code>resultsOf</code></a> hands back the values
            alone, a failed call left out.</p>
        <Code language="abide" caption="abide" code={`
            <script>
            import { chat } from '@abide/abide'
            import askAssistant from './askAssistant.server.ts'
            import searchContacts from './searchContacts.server.ts'

            const talk = chat({ ask: askAssistant })
            </script>

            {#for call of talk.calls(searchContacts) key call.toolUse.id}
                {#for contact of call.value ?? []}<p>{contact.name}</p>{/for}
            {/for}
        `} />
    </Section>

    <Section title="Send a picture or a statement in the question">
        <p>A turn is the Messages API's own blocks. A
            <a href={url('/headless/dropzone')}><code>dropzone</code></a> takes the files pasted
            or dropped, and <a href={url('/ai/assistant-message')}><code>assistantMessage</code></a>
            makes the person's message of the question and them: a photo drawn down to what a
            model looks at, a PDF as a document.</p>
        <Code language="abide" caption="abide" code={`
            <script>
            import { assistantMessage, dropzone, reactive } from '@abide/abide'

            let files = reactive<File[]>([])
            const drop = dropzone({
                accept: 'image/*,application/pdf',
                paste: true,
                onfiles: (taken) => files.patch((all) => all.push(...taken)),
            })
            const asked = (question: string) =>
                assistantMessage('user', [...files, question])
            </script>

            <textarea {...drop.host}></textarea>
        `} />
        <p>Send a phone's HEIC as it is, and the assistant refuses it before any model is
            asked. <code>assistantMessage</code> redraws one the browser can draw as a JPEG.</p>
        <Exchange
            method="POST"
            path="/assistant/askAssistant"
            body={transcript([
                {
                    role: 'user',
                    content: [
                        {
                            type: 'image',
                            source: {
                                type: 'base64',
                                media_type: 'image/heic',
                                data: 'AAAA',
                            },
                        },
                        { type: 'text', text: 'Add this contact.' },
                    ],
                },
            ])} />
    </Section>
</Doc>
